# Match patterns # {#match-patterns}

A <dfn>match pattern</dfn> is a [=string=] used to match URLs.

A match pattern is either the special value `&lt;all_urls&gt;`, or a string of the
form:

```
&lt;scheme&gt;://&lt;host&gt;&lt;path&gt;
```

Issue: This section documents the grammar and matching algorithm as
implemented by Chromium, Gecko, and WebKit today. Where the engines
disagree, the disagreement is called out in an `Issue:` rather than
resolved, since resolving it is a normative decision for the group.

## Grammar ## {#match-pattern-grammar}

### Scheme ### {#match-pattern-scheme}

|scheme| is one of a fixed list of literal scheme names, or `*`.

A pattern's |scheme| is the substring of the match pattern before the
first `://`.

Issue: Chromium and WebKit accept `http`, `https`, `file`, `ftp`, plus
their own extension scheme, as literal |scheme| values in `matches` /
`host_permissions`. Gecko additionally accepts `ws`, `wss`, and `data`.
Chromium additionally accepts `ws`/`wss` for `host_permissions` (but not
for `content_scripts.matches`) and its own `chrome://` WebUI scheme
(policy/enterprise contexts only). WebKit accepts none of `ws`, `wss`,
`data`, or a WebUI-equivalent scheme in a match pattern at all. The group
needs to decide the portable literal-scheme set, if any, versus treating
each additional scheme as engine-specific.

If |scheme| is `*`, the pattern matches a fixed, engine-defined set of
schemes in place of a literal scheme.

Issue: The `*` scheme wildcard does not expand to the same set of schemes
in every engine. Chromium and WebKit expand it to `http` and `https`
only. Gecko expands it to `http`, `https`, `ws`, and `wss`. The group
needs to pick one expansion.

### Host ### {#match-pattern-host}

|host| is one of:

* `*`, matching any host.
* `*.` followed by a domain suffix (containing no `*` or `/`), matching
    that domain and any of its subdomains.
* a literal domain or IP-address literal (containing no `*` or `/`),
    matching only that exact host.

A `*` may not appear anywhere else in |host|; a pattern containing one is
invalid.

When |scheme| is `file`, |host| is omitted (the pattern has the form
`file://&lt;path&gt;` or `file:///&lt;path&gt;`, with `&lt;path&gt;` beginning at the third
slash regardless of what, if anything, appears between the second and
third slash).

Host comparison is case-insensitive.

Issue: Whether host comparison is *by design* case-insensitive differs
by engine, though the observable result is close. WebKit folds ASCII
case explicitly when comparing hosts. Chromium's comparison is a plain
byte comparison, but both the pattern's host and the URL's host are
independently canonicalized to lowercase before the comparison runs, so
it is case-insensitive as a side effect of canonicalization rather than
by an explicit fold in the comparison itself. No case-folding call was
found in Gecko's host comparison; an intentionally-uppercase literal host
in a Gecko match pattern therefore does not match a real (lowercase) URL
host. If the group specifies case-insensitive host matching normatively,
this is (as far as could be determined from source) a required behavior
change for Gecko, not merely a documentation fix.

Issue: Only Chromium canonicalizes a Unicode (non-ASCII) |host| to
Punycode/ASCII-Compatible-Encoding at parse time. Neither Gecko nor
WebKit were found to do this. A match pattern host written with literal
Unicode characters will therefore fail to match the corresponding
navigated URL (whose host a user agent always Punycode-encodes) in Gecko
and WebKit, unless the extension author writes the Punycode form
themselves. The group should decide whether pattern hosts are required
to be pre-encoded ASCII, or whether Unicode host canonicalization is
required of every engine.

A port is never written in |host|.

Issue: The three engines disagree sharply on what happens if an author
writes one anyway (e.g. `http://example.com:8080/*`). Chromium accepts a
`&lt;host&gt;:&lt;port&gt;` form as a distinct, real grammar component: the pattern
then matches only URLs on that exact port, and omitting the port (the
common case) matches any port. Gecko has no port grammar at all: the
`:8080` text is silently absorbed into the literal host string, which
then can never match any real (portless) host, so the pattern becomes
permanently unmatchable without any parse error. WebKit explicitly
rejects any pattern containing port syntax in the host as invalid at
parse time. The group needs to decide whether match patterns support a
port component at all and, if not, whether writing one should be a
parse error (WebKit's behavior) or ignored, rather than silently
unmatchable (current Gecko behavior).

<div class="example">
`https://*.example.com/*` matches `https://example.com/`,
`https://www.example.com/`, and `https://a.b.example.com/`, but not
`https://notexample.com/`.
</div>

### Path ### {#match-pattern-path}

|path| begins with `/` and is required; a pattern with no |path| (not
even a bare `/`) is invalid.

|path| may contain any number of `*` characters, each matching zero or
more characters. No other character in |path| has special meaning; in
particular, unlike a [=glob=], `?` is an ordinary literal character in a
match pattern's |path|, not a single-character wildcard.

Path matching is case-sensitive.

Issue: The current text of this section previously stated that match
patterns "are case-insensitive" as a blanket claim. That is not accurate
for the path component in any of the three engines: Chromium, Gecko, and
WebKit all compare |path| case-sensitively in their production code
paths (Chromium exposes a case-insensitive matching mode in its API, but
no call site in the browser's own permission or content-script matching
code uses it).

Issue: Whether the query string is included in what |path| matches
against differs. Chromium and Gecko both match |path| against the URL's
path plus its query string (with the fragment excluded either way).
WebKit's source only reads the URL's path accessor, not its query
accessor, when matching, meaning a WebKit match pattern's |path| appears
to be compared against the path alone. This was not confirmed against
WebKit by an executed test with a literal (unescaped) query separator,
only inferred from which accessor the matching code calls, so the group
should verify this directly against a build before relying on it.

Issue: Percent-encoding is handled inconsistently. Chromium decodes
percent-encoded octets in both the URL and the pattern's |path| before
comparing (falling back to a raw comparison for byte sequences that
aren't valid UTF-8). WebKit performs no decoding at all: a pattern
containing a literal, unescaped reserved character will not match a URL
where the user agent encoded that same character. Whether Gecko decodes
was not determined from `MatchPattern.cpp` alone.

<div class="example">
`https://example.com/foo/*` matches `https://example.com/foo/`,
`https://example.com/foo/bar`, and `https://example.com/foo/bar?baz`,
but not `https://example.com/foobar` or `https://example.com/foo`
(without a trailing slash).
</div>

## <dfn export>`&lt;all_urls&gt;`</dfn> ## {#all-urls}

The special match pattern `&lt;all_urls&gt;` matches all URLs whose scheme is
in a permitted set of schemes, regardless of host or path.

Issue: The permitted scheme set for `&lt;all_urls&gt;` is the largest
three-way divergence found in this area, and it is not merely a
different literal list; the *shape* of the set differs.

* Chromium's set depends on which manifest surface is asking. For
    `content_scripts.matches`, `&lt;all_urls&gt;` covers `http`, `https`,
    `file`, `ftp`, and the isolated-web-app UUID scheme (plus `chrome://`
    WebUI pages, gated behind an experimental flag). For
    `host_permissions`, the set additionally includes `ws` and `wss`.
    Neither surface includes `data:` or the extension's own scheme.
* Gecko uses one fixed set for every surface: `http`, `https`, `ws`,
    `wss`, `file`, `ftp`, and `data`. This is the only one of the three
    engines that includes `data:` in `&lt;all_urls&gt;`.
* WebKit's set is the narrowest by far: `http`, `https`, and the
    extension's own scheme. `file:` is matched only if the caller has
    separately opted in to allowing file access; by default it is
    excluded. `ftp`, `ws`, `wss`, and `data` are never included.

The group needs to choose a portable `&lt;all_urls&gt;` scheme set (or
explicitly leave `file:` and other non-http(s) schemes as an
engine-specific opt-in, matching WebKit's model, rather than an
unconditional inclusion as in Gecko's model).

<div class="example">
Given a `content_scripts.matches` list of `["&lt;all_urls&gt;"]`: a content
script runs on `https://example.com/` in all three engines; runs on
`ftp://example.com/` in Chromium and Gecko but not WebKit; runs on
`data:text/html,...` in Gecko only; and runs on `file:///home/user/x.html`
in Chromium and Gecko, but only in WebKit if file access has separately
been granted.
</div>

## Matching algorithm ## {#match-pattern-matching-algorithm}

To determine whether a [=match pattern=] |pattern| matches a URL |url|:

1. Let |url record| be the result of parsing |url|.
1. If |pattern| is `&lt;all_urls&gt;`:
    1. If the [=scheme=] of |url record| is in the permitted scheme set
        for `&lt;all_urls&gt;` (see above), return true.
    1. Otherwise, return false.
1. If the [=scheme=] of |url record| does not match |pattern|'s |scheme|
    component (a literal scheme must be equal; `*` must be one of the
    schemes it expands to for the current context), return false.
1. If the [=host=] of |url record| does not match |pattern|'s |host|
    component, return false.
1. If |pattern|'s |scheme| is not `file`, and |pattern|'s |path|
    component does not match the [=path=] of |url record| (see the path
    matching notes above for what "path" includes), return false.
1. Return true.

This algorithm is run against the URL produced by
[Determine the URL for matching a document](https://w3c.github.io/webextensions/specification/#determine-the-url-for-matching-a-document) when matching a document
for `content_scripts`, not necessarily against the document's literal
URL; it does not itself define how an opaque-origin document's URL is
resolved.

Issue: Gecko additionally supports a stricter "explicit" matching mode
(used for `host_permissions`-style checks as opposed to
`content_scripts.matches`-style checks) in which a pattern that matches
subdomains (a leading `*.` or bare `*` host) never matches, regardless of
the URL. No equivalent mode was found in Chromium's or WebKit's
match-pattern matching code itself. The group should decide whether this
"explicit host" restriction belongs in this section as a named concept,
or is purely a permission-UI concern out of scope for match pattern
matching itself.

<div class="example">
`*://*.example.com/*` matches `https://www.example.com/`,
`http://example.com/`, and `wss://example.com/` in Gecko, but only the
first two in Chromium and WebKit, since Gecko's `*` scheme wildcard
additionally covers `ws`/`wss`.
</div>
